    "iast": "vimalakalāśrayābhinavasṛṣṭimahājananī bharitatanuśca pañcamukhaguptarucirjanakaḥ |\ntadubhayayāmalasphuritabhāvavisargamayaṃ hṛdayamanuttarāmṛtakulaṃ mama saṃsphuratāt || 1 ||",
    "translation": "May my Heart shine forth, which is the supreme nectar-like totality (Kula), formed by the emission of the state pulsating from the intimate union of both [the Mother and the Father]—the great Mother who is the support of the pure energies and the new creation, and the Father whose body is completely full and who delights in the five faces.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nThis Mangalacarana (invocation) carries, per the source's own table of contents, roughly 28 pages of commentary on its own — more than any other single verse in the chapter. Abhinavagupta builds it on a deliberate double meaning (shlesha): every phrase simultaneously names his human parents (his mother Vimala, 'the pure one,' and his father Narasimhagupta) and the cosmic parents Shakti and Shiva. Anuttara, the key term in the closing line, means literally 'that which has nothing superior to it' — the Absolute, elsewhere defined (in Abhinavagupta's own Paratrimshika-vivarana) as consciousness that is self-luminous and needs no other, higher perceiver to know it. Visarga, usually just 'emission,' names the paradox at the heart of this theology: Shiva's creative outpouring is simultaneously perfect, motionless stillness, since nothing outside himself is ever actually produced — the translator Alexis Sanderson accordingly renders it 'the state of absolute potential.' Jayaratha glosses 'father...whose body is full' (bharitatanu) as an epithet applied directly to Bhairava elsewhere in the tradition (compare Vijnanabhairava verses 15 and 23, bharitakara, 'of full form'), and unpacks the 'five faces' as the five powers of consciousness, bliss, will, knowledge, and action — mapped in Abhinavagupta's own Malinivijayavarttika (MVV 1/169cd-171ab) onto the five traditional faces of Sadashiva: Tatpurusha (east), Sadyojata (west), Vamadeva (north), Aghora (south), and Ishana (above). The commentary also stages Trika's core disagreement with rival causal theories here at the very outset: unlike Advaita Vedanta, which treats the diverse world as born of ignorance superimposed on one undivided Brahman, or Samkhya, which derives the world from an unconscious Nature (prakriti) so the soul can exhaust past karma, Trika holds that Shiva himself, purely through his own freedom (svatantrya), becomes everything without being limited by any of it — not merely underlying the many as their hidden true nature (as Vedanta's Brahman does), but actively and freely becoming each one.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nThink of this as the ultimate 'thank you' note, working on two levels at once. On one level, Abhinavagupta thanks his actual mother and father for his birth. On the other, at exactly the same time, using the same words, he's thanking the ultimate creative principle of the universe — Shiva and Shakti — for existing at all. He's asking his own 'Heart' (not the organ, but the innermost core of awareness) to wake up and shine, using a term for the Absolute, Anuttara, that literally means 'nothing beats this' — there's nothing higher, nothing it depends on, nothing outside it.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nEverything in life has this same dual nature: the physical (our literal parents, our biology, the ordinary facts of how we got here) and the spiritual (the deeper creative energy that animates all of it). True spiritual awakening doesn't mean rejecting or transcending the physical, ordinary story of how you came to exist — it means learning to see the divine operating perfectly through exactly that story, without needing a different, more 'spiritual-sounding' one.",
