    "iast": "naumi citpratibhāṃ devīṃ parāṃ bhairavayoginīm |\nmātṛmānaprameyāṃśaśūlāmbujakṛtāspadām || 2 ||",
    "translation": "I bow to the Goddess who is the creative intuition of consciousness (Cit-Pratibha), the supreme Yogini of Bhairava, who makes her home in the lotus that sits atop the trident of the perceiver, the perception, and the perceived.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nThis opens the invocation of the Trika triad of goddesses proper: Para (Supreme, praised here), Parapara (Middling, verse 4), and Apara (Lower, verse 3) — corresponding respectively to Shiva's powers of will, knowledge, and action, and mapped onto the classical fourfold scheme of valid cognition (pramana) inherited from the Nyayabhashya (1/1/1): pramatr (the knowing subject), pramana (the means of knowing), prameya (the known object), and pramiti (the resulting valid cognition itself). Para is identified with all four at once — she is 'full' (purna) precisely because subject, means, object, and result of knowing are all, ultimately, phases of her own single nature. Pratibha, translated here 'creative intuition,' is a recurring technical term (see also 3/68, 10/209-211, 11/76-78 in the source) for the spontaneous, pre-conceptual flash of insight that precedes and grounds discursive thought — Abhinavagupta opens his separate commentary on the Paratrimshika with an almost identical verse praising Para as 'the wonder of the intuitive imagination' (pratibha-camatkriti). The verse also introduces the Trika Trident (trishula) mandala, elaborated at length in Chapter 31: three rays rise from Sadashiva's navel — named Shakti, Vyapini ('the Pervasive'), and Samana ('the Equal') — terminating in three white lotuses of the Transmental (unmana) at the crown, on which Para, Parapara, and Apara sit enthroned with their respective consorts. Jayaratha stresses that although Para is one of the three, she is also all three together, since the whole triad is simply her single power of consciousness manifesting through three polarities — an early, concrete instance of Abhinavagupta's central hermeneutical device, the 'Supreme Nonduality' (paramadvaya) in which every part already contains the whole.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nImagine looking at an apple: there's you (the looker), the act of looking (your eyesight), and the apple (the object) — and, this verse adds, the actual moment of successfully seeing it. Old Indian logic (Nyaya) already had this fourfold breakdown of any act of knowing. Abhinavagupta's move is to say: all four of these are really just one Goddess, appearing in four different roles at once. That's Para — not one piece of the knowing-process, but the whole process, seen as alive, conscious, and single.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nWhenever you feel fragmented — like the 'you' who's aware, the thing you're aware of, and the awareness itself are three separate pieces bolted together — it's worth remembering this older idea that they were never actually separate to begin with. The apparent three-way (or four-way) split is just consciousness looking at itself from different angles in the same instant.",
