    "iast": "naumi devīṃ śarīrasthāṃ nṛtyato bhairavākṛteḥ |\nprāvṛṇmeghaghanavyomavidyullekhāvilāsinīm || 3 ||",
    "translation": "I bow to the goddess Apara, who dwells within the dancing body of Bhairava's form, playing there like a flash of lightning within a sky thick with monsoon storm clouds.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nApara, the 'Lower' goddess of the Trika triad, governs the level of duality and objectivity — hence her placement, in most Trika iconographic schemes, on the left prong of the trident, colored black, in contrast to red Parapara on the right and white Para at the center (see 15/323-325 and 15/334-335 in the source for the full color and placement scheme). 'Bhairava's dancing form' draws directly on Shiva Sutra 3.9's declaration atma nartakah, 'the Self is a dancer' — Kshemaraja's commentary on that aphorism explains the image as the Self playing out an endless variety of roles (bhumika) rooted in a freely self-imposed veiling of its own true nature, exactly the movement Jayaratha invokes here. The verse's central image is precise rather than merely decorative: 'dense storm clouds' (meghaghana) is Bhairava's own body — dark, thick, undifferentiated, the very picture of unindividuated Maya — while the 'lightning flash' (vidyullekha) is Apara herself, the instantaneous flash of divine light that appears within that darkness, illuminating one particular thing at one particular place and moment, then vanishing, only to flash again elsewhere. This is consciousness's power of action (kriyashakti) exactly as it operates in ordinary, moment-to-moment perception: sudden, local, and self-effacing, never remaining fixed anywhere, yet each flash fully being the one light.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nPicture the night sky during a monsoon storm: an enormous, thick, dark cloud bank (that's Bhairava's own vast, dancing body) is suddenly split by a single bright flash of lightning (that's the goddess Apara). The flash doesn't last, and it doesn't need to — it's brilliant and total in that one instant, in that one place. This is Abhinavagupta's way of describing ordinary perception and action: momentary, local, always shifting, yet each flash is genuinely, fully the same light.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nOrdinary, fleeting moments of clarity or action don't need to be permanent to be complete. A single flash of understanding, fully present just once, can carry the same essential light as anything more lasting — the value isn't diminished by how briefly it appears.",
