    "iast": "dīptajyotiśchaṭāpluṣṭabhedabandhatrayaṃ sphurat |\nstāj jñānaśūlaṃ satpakṣavipakṣotkartanakṣamam || 4 ||",
    "translation": "May the pulsingly radiant prong of knowledge — Parapara — that has burnt up the triple fetter with its blazing rays of the light of Shiva-consciousness, have the power to uproot everything opposed to the truth.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nParapara, occupying the knowledge-prong of the trident (generally the right prong, colored red), is defined by direct citation of the source scripture: 'the power of knowledge is what makes known, of any given thing, that it is thus and not otherwise' (Malinivijayottara 3/7). Because knowledge, will, and action are each said to inwardly contain the other two — the three prongs are 'fused' aspects of one triad (compare 3/108 in the source) — Parapara is not knowledge in isolation but knowledge shot through with will and action as well. The 'triple fetter' she burns away is the doctrine of the three malas (impurities) that will be formally introduced starting at verse 22 and developed at length from 9/60 onward: anava-mala (the root sense of incompleteness, treating oneself as a limited individual), mayiya-mala (the sense of duality and separateness from other things and beings), and karma-mala (the compulsion to act binding activity that follows from the other two). 'What runs contrary to the truth' is glossed with reference to the doctrine of the Six Blisses (shad-ananda, detailed at 5/44-52), ranging from ordinary worldly bliss up to Cosmic Bliss (jagadananda) — every degree short of that highest bliss counts as 'opposition' to be uprooted, not because it is evil, but because it is incomplete.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nParapara is described as a blazing spearpoint of pure discernment — the specific power that burns away three deep-seated chains people don't usually notice they're wearing: feeling fundamentally incomplete, seeing yourself as cut off from everything else, and being stuck on an endless treadmill of compulsive action. This verse asks that same fire to also cut down anything that stands in the way of the fullest possible experience of bliss, not just ordinary happiness but the total, cosmic kind.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nReal insight tends to work like fire on very specific things: it doesn't destroy indiscriminately, it burns away exactly the false, limiting self-descriptions a person has been carrying — 'I'm not enough,' 'I'm separate,' 'I have no choice' — while leaving everything genuine intact.",
