    "iast": "svātantryaśaktiḥ kramasaṃsisṛkṣā\nkramātmatā ceti vibhor vibhūtiḥ |\ntad eva devītrayam antarāstām\nanuttaraṃ me prathayat svarūpam || 5 ||",
    "translation": "May that same triad of Goddesses - who are the glorious power of the All-pervading Lord as 1) the power which is His freedom, 2) the intent to emit the succession of manifestation, and 3) the succession itself - inwardly reveal my own unsurpassable Anuttara nature.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nThis verse restates the Para/Parapara/Apara triad from a different angle — as stages of a single unfolding, rather than as fixed cognitive roles — and the source notes two genuinely different scholarly ways of mapping the correspondence. Jayaratha equates the three with the Trika principle of Shiva, Shakti, and Nara: Shiva's freedom as pure oneness, the will to emit as unity-in-difference, and the resulting world-process itself as duality — citing the Ishvarapratyabhijna's definition of temporal and spatial succession (krama) as grounded in relative distinction (IP 2/1/5). Anandabhatta, commenting separately on the Vijnanabhairava (which quotes this very verse), instead equates the three more simply and directly with Para, Apara, and Parapara themselves, in that order. Both readings agree, however, on the key term Anuttara, glossed here (echoing the Paratrimshika-laghuvritti) as consciousness so 'without-superior' that it needs no external, higher witness to establish its own reality — self-luminous and self-validating by definition. Notably, Abhinavagupta opens his separate, later commentary on the Paratrimshika with a structurally near-identical verse, invoking Para to reveal the same triad of 'Man, Shakti, and Shiva' within the Heart — suggesting this specific triadic invocation was something of a signature opening gesture for him across multiple works.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nReality unfolds, this verse says, in three moves: first, there's absolute, undirected freedom; second, that freedom forms an intention to create a sequence of things; third, the actual sequence — the world as we experience it, one thing after another — comes into being. Interestingly, different traditional commentators line up exactly which goddess matches which of these three moves slightly differently, which is a good reminder that even within one tradition, the fine details of symbolic mapping were genuinely debated, not fixed dogma.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nMost real creative acts follow this same three-step shape: an open field of possibility, a specific intention forming within it, and then the actual sequence of steps that intention plays out into the world. Recognizing which of the three stages you're currently in can clarify what kind of effort actually helps — freedom doesn't need forcing, intention needs clarifying, and sequence needs following through.",
