    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nGanesha is invoked as cakreshvara, 'Lord of the Wheel' — specifically, the wheel of the senses and life-energies (the 'great rays' issuing from Para, Parapara, and Apara). Jayaratha pairs him here with his brother Vatuka, both being 'sons of the Goddess': Vatuka governs the exhaled breath (prana) and is likened to the sun, while Ganesha governs the inhaled breath (apana) and is likened to the moon — explaining the verse's specific epithet 'shining with the beauty of the Moon.' A separate, parallel image describes ordinary sensory experience itself as an inner 'sun' with twelve rays (the intellect, mind, and the five organs each of action and perception, following the Samkhya's own count of the senses) — when this solar ego turns fully inward rather than staying fixed on external objects, it recognizes every object as nothing but its own light returning to itself, an idea developed further at 9/233 onward in the source. This verse's placement is itself conventional: Ganesha (as Vinayaka, 'remover — or, if unpropitiated, creator — of obstacles') is invoked near the start of a great many Sanskrit treatises for exactly this reason, but Abhinavagupta characteristically reinterprets the convention in specifically Trika terms rather than leaving it as an unexamined formality.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nGanesha here isn't just the familiar elephant-headed remover of obstacles — he's described as the actual governor of your senses and breath, specifically your inhale, paired with a 'sun' brother who governs the exhale. Abhinavagupta is saying: before any big undertaking, first bring your scattered senses and breath into harmony under this single, moonlike, unifying presence, and only then will the deeper ocean of awareness underneath them be able to rise and stir.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nBefore starting serious, focused work, it helps to first settle and unify what's most immediate and physical — breath, senses, scattered attention — rather than trying to leap straight to deep insight while still internally fragmented. Coherence at the simplest level tends to be the precondition for anything deeper to surface.",
