    "translation": "Victory to the ancient teachers of the lineage called Traiyambaka — radiant as a heap of flawless pearls gathered from the Tamraparni river — who steer the boat, unerring helmsmen playing amid the heaving waves of the great ocean that is the teachers' scripture.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nBehind this verse stands a full mythic history that Jayaratha quotes at length from Somananda's own Shivadrishti (ShD 7/107-122): when the Shaiva revelation neared extinction with the passing of the ancient rishis in the Kali Age, Shiva descended to earth in the mortal form of Shrikantha and commanded the sage Durvasa to revive it. Durvasa, in turn, mind-generated (not physically fathered) a son, Tryambakaditya, founder of the lineage called Traiyambaka (vernacular: Teramba). For fourteen generations this lineage propagated itself the same way — each accomplished Siddha mentally generating a son before flying up into the sky — until the fifteenth took an ordinary human wife, after which the line continued by natural descent through Samgamaditya, Varshaditya, Arunaditya, and Ananda, down to Somananda himself, author of the Shivadrishti. Jayaratha notes that alongside Tryambaka, two other teachers, Amardaka (dualist Shaivism) and Shrinatha (dual-cum-nondual Shaivism), were commissioned in the same way, giving rise to three orders (mathika, literally 'little monastery,' the term for a Shaiva teaching-lineage's institutional center) — plus a fourth, 'half' lineage (Ardha-traiyambaka) transmitted through Tryambaka's own daughter, on the Kaula premise that women are innately more spiritually advanced and so require an abbreviated ('half') initiation. It is specifically this daughter's lineage, not the male line that produced Somananda's own Pratyabhijna school, through which Trika itself later reached Abhinavagupta via Shambhunatha. The commentary flags a genuine textual-critical puzzle here too: the modern scholar Nemec has noted that most manuscripts of the Shivadrishti actually omit this origin-story passage, raising the possibility it was appended later — yet Jayaratha and the whole Kashmiri tradition treat it as authoritative, and Abhinavagupta himself alludes to the same account elsewhere (in his Ishvarapratyabhijnavivrtivimarshini).\n\n**Beginner Note**\nBehind the poetic image of 'pearls fished from a river' and 'expert boat-captains' sits an entire legendary family tree: Shiva himself came down as a teacher named Shrikantha, commissioned a sage to revive the dying tradition, and that sage mentally 'gave birth' to a son the same way you might have an idea — no ordinary conception involved. This happened fourteen times in a row before one of these mind-born teachers finally married an actual human wife and the lineage became a normal family line, ending several generations later in Somananda, one of Abhinavagupta's most important predecessors. And notably, one whole branch of this family tradition — the one that eventually taught Abhinavagupta himself — passed specifically through a daughter, not a son.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nEven a tradition steeped in mythic, larger-than-life origin stories still cared about being able to trace an actual chain of transmission, teacher to teacher, generation to generation. It's worth noticing how much weight both the myth and the very concrete genealogical bookkeeping carry side by side here — grand origin stories and careful record-keeping about who actually taught whom aren't necessarily at odds.",
